WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.659
All around Britain, you will find this:
tactile paving.

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:06.560
It’s to help blind and partially sighted
people find their way around.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.080
And here is the clever part,

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.700
and the part that almost everyone who is fully
sighted will never have noticed:

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.620
there are different patterns for different
situations.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.710
Very few people see nothing at all.

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:19.870
That’s important to say. Only about 3%.

00:00:19.870 --> 00:00:22.920
Some people have light perception, some people
have a bit more vision.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.940
Some people have a limited field of vision.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.119
Some people have poor central vision but good
peripheral vision.

00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:33.440
Essentially, you think of partially sighted
people with enough vision

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.130
to be able to get around safely and independently,

00:00:37.130 --> 00:00:39.679
blind being below that threshold.

00:00:39.679 --> 00:00:42.969
Dots in a grid, like this, mean a dropped
kerb for crossing the road.

00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:45.530
If it’s red, or at least, if it’s supposed
to be red,

00:00:45.530 --> 00:00:48.570
it’s a crossing with lights or something
else to stop traffic:

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.639
these designs are for partially sighted people
too,

00:00:50.639 --> 00:00:54.389
so bright contrasting colours are a good idea.

00:00:54.389 --> 00:00:58.379
There is a small amount of tactile paving
here, used as a marker,

00:00:58.379 --> 00:01:00.030
to flag up where the crossing is.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.499
And then near the road, it’s for the full
length of the crossing,

00:01:02.499 --> 00:01:03.979
so you can work out where it is.

00:01:03.979 --> 00:01:06.500
And it’s important to balance marking the
crossing

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.450
against not using too much tactile paving:

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:13.010
because someone with arthritis can find surfaces
like this painful to cross.

00:01:13.010 --> 00:01:19.130
Navigating around an unfamiliar area is always
a bit scary, to be honest.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:24.600
Because, obviously, traditional visual clues,
landmarks etc, aren't there.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.030
It helps a lot if clues are consistent,

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:32.210
so confusion can certainly occur when that
isn’t the case.

00:01:32.210 --> 00:01:38.020
It’s very, very important that these adaptations
that are there to help us

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:39.799
are actually there to help us

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:43.200
and are actually there in the way that we
expect to see them.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.890
But there are other patterns too.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:49.530
Offset dots mean there’s a train platform
ahead, a big chasm you could fall into.

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:51.460
Lozenge shapes mean a tram platform,

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:54.149
something closer to street level that could
run you over.

00:01:54.149 --> 00:01:56.780
Stripes across the path mean there’s some
steps ahead,

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.909
or a couple of other things that could trip
you up.

00:01:58.909 --> 00:02:02.150
Stripes along the path mean a safe route to
follow.

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:05.090
And if there’s a path that’s half for
foot traffic, and half for bicycles?

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.610
Well, the direction of the stripes tells you
which side is which.

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:12.360
Boroughs in London, councils outside of London,

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.300
seem to be moving more towards
aesthetic considerations,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:21.360
i.e. they’re changing the colour to darker
greys so it blends in more.

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.170
By definition if it blends in more,

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:27.320
it’s harder for partially sighted people
such as myself to actually see.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:29.820
Or obviously, in a more dire situation,

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:34.220
people can find themselves in the road, not
knowing they’re in the road,

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.420
simply because those essential clues aren’t
there.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.920
And sure, all this is tricky to get right.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:44.890
The design standards for how this is laid
down are literally a hundred pages long.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:48.420
But so are the design standards for everything
about public infrastructure:

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:52.200
having reliable rules that everyone understands
is important

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:55.040
when there are tons of metal speeding past
you.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:59.940
Thank you very much to the team from the
Royal National Institute of Blind People for all their help!

00:02:59.950 --> 00:03:01.460
You can check out their YouTube channel here,

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.200
and yes, I have linked the full hundred-page design
document in the description!

